  "model": "gemini-4-carbon",
  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: Before moving in, the Tenant shall give the Landlord twelve post-dated cheques covering the first year's rent.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:10:30.612842Z",
 "text": "No, this clause is not enforceable. Under section 108(a) of Ontario's *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, neither a landlord nor a tenancy agreement may require a tenant or prospective tenant to provide post-dated cheques or other negotiable instruments for the payment of rent. While a tenant and landlord can mutually and voluntarily agree to use post-dated cheques as a payment method, a lease clause mandating them is contrary to the Act and therefore void.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 108",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
